==========================================================================================
A  THE THREE IDENTITIES, SYMBOLICALLY, WITH THEIR CORRECT BOUNDARY TERMS
==========================================================================================

I1   qdot^T H q      with H SYMMETRIC
     d/dt[ (1/2) q^T H q ] - qdot^T H q  =  0
     => INTEGRAL qdot^T H q dt = (1/2) [ q^T H q ] evaluated at the ENDPOINTS.
     BOUNDARY CONDITION: the POSITIONS must return, q(T)^T H q(T) = q(0)^T H q(0).
     *** NOT "qdot = 0 at both ends". The draft justified this identity with the
     *** wrong condition. Part C measures which one the records actually satisfy.

I1a  the same integrand with H ANTISYMMETRIC is NOT a total derivative
     (1/2) q^T H_antisym q  =  0   (identically zero, so it cannot be the antiderivative)
     and the integrand itself is not identically zero, so ONLY the symmetric part of
     H is annihilated. That is exactly what makes the table a SYMMETRY condition.

I2   qddot^T H q      (any H)
     d/dt[ qdot^T H q ] - qddot^T H q - qdot^T H qdot  =  0
     => INTEGRAL qddot^T H q dt = [ qdot^T H q ] - INTEGRAL qdot^T H qdot dt.
     BOUNDARY CONDITION: qdot = 0 at both ends. The surviving term is a QUADRATIC
     FORM in velocity, which does not vanish for a generic trajectory.

I3   qddot^T H qdot   with H SYMMETRIC
     d/dt[ (1/2) qdot^T H qdot ] - qddot^T H qdot  =  0
     => INTEGRAL qddot^T H qdot dt = (1/2) [ qdot^T H qdot ] at the endpoints.
     BOUNDARY CONDITION: qdot = 0 at both ends. Correct in the draft.

  A verdict: all three identities verified symbolically

==========================================================================================
B  THE NINE CELLS: which identity each reduces to, and the H whose symmetry is needed
==========================================================================================

  Setup. The inner product on the protected rows is, with D = diag(1/std_x[3:6]^2) constant and
  W = M^-T D M^-1,

      <J_j, Delta>  =  -Ts^2 * SUM_k  r_j(k)^T W f_a(k),
      r_j = (dM_j) qddot + (dC_j) qdot + (dK_j) q.

  Sect. 8.2 measured that exactly one of the three terms of r_j survives per parameter family,
  so each cell is a single product. Substituting the three addition types gives the table.

  addition          family term           integrand         H               id  
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  f_a = -K_a q      mass  (dM_j) qddot    qddot^T H q       (dM_j) W K_a    I2  
                    NOT automatic: leaves -SUM qdot^T H qdot
  f_a = -K_a q      damping (dC_j) qdot   qdot^T H q        (dC_j) W K_a    I1  
                    ZERO if H symmetric AND positions return
  f_a = -K_a q      stiffness (dK) q      q^T H q           (dK) W K_a      --  
                    NOT automatic: a quadratic form in q
  f_a = -C_a qdot   mass  (dM_j) qddot    qddot^T H qdot    (dM_j) W C_a    I3  
                    ZERO if H symmetric AND qdot = 0 at the ends
  f_a = -C_a qdot   damping (dC_j) qdot   qdot^T H qdot     (dC_j) W C_a    --  
                    NEVER zero for a dissipative C_a: positive form
  f_a = -C_a qdot   stiffness (dK) q      q^T H qdot        (dK) W C_a      I1  
                    ZERO if H symmetric AND positions return
  f_a = -M_a qddot  mass  (dM_j) qddot    qddot^T H qddot   (dM_j) W M_a    --  
                    NOT automatic: a quadratic form in qddot
  f_a = -M_a qddot  damping (dC_j) qdot   qdot^T H qddot    (dC_j) W M_a    I3  
                    ZERO if H symmetric AND qdot = 0 at the ends
  f_a = -M_a qddot  stiffness (dK) q      q^T H qddot       (dK) W M_a      I2  
                    NOT automatic: leaves -SUM qdot^T H qdot

  CORRECTION TO THE DRAFT TABLE. Two cells were justified with the wrong boundary condition:
  (reactive addition vs the damping family) and (dissipative addition vs the stiffness family)
  both reduce to I1, which needs the POSITIONS to return, not the velocities to vanish. Their
  entries in Sect. 8.4 stand, but the hypothesis they rest on is different from the other two
  zero cells, and it is a weaker one on this data. Part C measures the gap.

  UNCHANGED AND CONFIRMED. The load-bearing cell for the lossless conclusion is
  (dissipative addition vs the damping family): it is SUM qdot^T (dC_j) W C_a qdot, a quadratic
  form in velocity with no boundary term and no symmetry escape. For a dissipative C_a it has a
  fixed sign and cannot vanish. That is the obstruction, and it does not depend on either
  boundary condition.


==========================================================================================
C  THE IDENTITIES ON THE REAL RECORDS (a discrete sum is not an integral)
==========================================================================================
  For each identity: |sum| is the discrete sum times ts, |bdy| is the exact boundary
  term the identity predicts, and |resid| = |sum - bdy| is the quadrature error.
  "rel" normalises the residual by the natural scale of the integrand, so it is the
  fraction of the integrand that does NOT cancel. That fraction is the accuracy any
  parity construction can achieve on this data.

  --- STANDSTILL (frozen Y)
  record / identity                 |sum|        |bdy|      |resid|          rel
  T1_standstill_ I1 qdot^T H q   4.5517e-06   5.1048e-06   5.5310e-07   3.9448e-06
  T1_standstill_ I2 qddot^T H q   2.5849e-04   4.5571e-04   1.9722e-04   1.1805e-06
  T1_standstill_ I3 qddot^T H qdot   4.8740e-04   3.2318e-04   1.6422e-04   7.6189e-06
